+# Source and prior-attempt gate: 30004656 / OWR-4990384-001
+
+The complete short Bubeck contribution is OWR15/2021 printed862, visually checked, with the organizers' summary on854. It explicitly points to Bubeck–Li–Nagaraj, arXiv2009.14444. Official report https://ems.press/content/serial-article-files/46893 ; report/workshop year2021, EMS publication15March2022. The short catalog sentence omits essential stochastic and architectural hypotheses; it is not a universal statement about every interpolated dataset.
+
+The precise source is BLN Conjecture1 in arXiv2009.14444v2 and the published COLT2021 paper, https://proceedings.mlr.press/v134/bubeck21a/bubeck21a.pdf . It considers a fixed Lipschitz activation ψ:R→R and f(x)=sum_{j=1}^k a_j ψ(w_j·x+b_j), with arbitrary real weights and biases. Inputs are independent uniform spherical points, or normalized Gaussian points N(0,I_d/d), and labels are independent random signs. The displayed lower bound is Lip_{S^{d-1}}(f)≥c sqrt(n/k) with high probability over the data, uniformly over interpolating networks. No numerical failure probability is fixed in the conjecture; its footnote even calls a positive-probability result interesting. The matching-construction Conjecture2 is separate and is not the assigned lower-bound question.
+
+There is a domain-convention issue to preserve: the original display uses the sphere-restricted Lipschitz norm even while the preceding generic-data definition also permits Gaussian inputs off the sphere. The paper explicitly distinguishes global or ball Lipschitz norms as weaker targets for its proved lower bounds. Later literature uses global/domain-appropriate norms for Gaussian data. This gate does not silently identify these norms or claim a solution from that issue. Author partials will state their actual input distribution and norm domain, beginning with the unambiguous spherical model.
+
+Primary prior results: the original paper proves spectral-weight proxies, a global-norm undercomplete/projection lower bound with d in place of n, and polynomial/tensor regimes, with their stated bias/domain conditions. Bubeck–Sellke, https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.12806 and NeurIPS2021, proves a broad isoperimetric robustness law for Lipschitz parameterizations with polynomial-size parameters; this does not remove arbitrary weights. The catalog's 'Wu–Sahai' attribution for arXiv2202.11592 is incorrect: the authors are Wu, Huang and Zhang. Their published ICML2023 result (https://proceedings.mlr.press/v202/wu23g/wu23g.pdf) retains polynomial-weight hypotheses.
+
+Shmalo's July2026 preprint https://arxiv.org/pdf/2607.07778v1 claims arbitrary-weight two-layer continuous piecewise-linear activation lower bounds up to one logarithmic factor, with d≥3 for its sphere rigidity argument, domain-appropriate Gaussian bounds, realized-kink refinements and additional projection results. Its full log-free/general-activation target is explicitly left unresolved. This recent preprint is not independently certified in full by this gate and is not a premise of the elementary partials below. Its exact downloaded bytes are preserved locally. No result for a restricted activation or parameter regime is promoted to the whole original conjecture, and no novelty/current-open certification is made.
+
+Exact ID and alias, overparameterization and robustness PR/commit/branch searches found no matching campaign attempt. All475 locally mirrored refs were searched by relevant commit messages; the semantic robustness match is a gamma-theta eternal-domination probe, unrelated to neural networks. The complete pinned record was read; its research_results alias is null. This is not exhaustive full-text reading of every Git blob. Gate eligible; retrieval counts zero turns. The original broad lower-bound question remains unresolved.
